  "title": "how much of a graph should be visible the moment it opens",
  "source": "plan-proposal",
  "source_detail": "Q4 of docs/plans/groups-in-the-layout/PLAN.md — what replaces fitToView's shrink-everything-to-one-screen rule",
  "explanation": "The page shrinks every graph to one screen when it opens, as far down as a third of normal size, and that is the cramming. [Never shrinking](#never-shrink) is the recommendation: a graph that already fits opens centred exactly as it does today, and one that does not opens readable with the rest reachable by panning. [Keeping a floor under the shrinking](#shrink-with-a-floor) rescues a graph that only just overflows and nothing larger, and [dropping the centring as well](#never-centre) buys one rule with no branch in it. Leaves out whether a control to fit everything on demand should replace what is being taken away — that is the last question.",
